<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1.0/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  CPU-Power's polkit action. THIS FILE IS PART OF THE SECURITY BOUNDARY.
  It is not configuration. It is the rule that says who may run the privileged helper, and every
  value in it is generated by CMake from the same variables that build the program, so the two
  cannot drift.

  WHY THE DEFAULTS ARE WHAT THEY ARE

    allow_any       no    anybody at all, including a remote login
    allow_inactive  no    a local session that is not the one currently on screen
    allow_active    yes   the person physically at this machine right now

  That triple is the whole security model, and it is the one thing a sudoers rule cannot
  express, which is why this project has no sudoers fallback at all any more. Adjusting
  the CPU power profile on the machine you are sitting at is not an administrative act. It is a
  preference, like screen brightness or volume, and every desktop settings panel treats it as one.
  Nothing here grants anything to anyone who is not at the keyboard.

  It also means this tool does not need to know what the session tracker reports for a remote or
  idle session, on any distribution: both of those buckets are denied by construction rather than
  by inspection. That is what makes the claim portable rather than tested-on-one-box.

  THE TWO ANNOTATIONS ARE THE PIN

  exec.path is compared against the program path BYTE FOR BYTE, with no canonicalisation: no
  realpath, no stat, no inode comparison. So it must be spelled exactly as the program spells it
  when it execs, which is why both come from @CPUPOWER_HELPER_PATH@ and neither is written by
  hand. If they ever drift nothing crashes: this action simply stops matching, pkexec falls back
  to org.freedesktop.policykit.exec, and the tool starts asking for an administrator password it
  was never meant to ask for.

  exec.argv1 pins argv[1] AND NOTHING AFTER IT. That is a real limit, not a formality: pkexec
  does no validation of the remaining arguments, so the helper itself must reject every argument
  it does not recognise, and scripts/sec-gate.sh asserts that it does.

  WHY THERE IS NO .rules FILE ANYWHERE IN THIS PROJECT

  polkit has two mechanisms and they belong to different people. This file, a .policy action,
  DECLARES what may be done and what the defaults are, and it is the application's to ship. A
  .rules file is JavaScript evaluated inside polkitd which OVERRIDES those defaults, and polkit(8)
  says applications "must never include any authorization rules", because that namespace belongs
  to the system administrator. An administrator may therefore override everything below, including
  turning it into a password challenge, and that is entirely their prerogative.

  NOTE FOR ANYONE EDITING THIS COMMENT: a literal double hyphen is not legal inside an XML
  comment. Writing one here does not produce an error at install time; it makes polkitd drop the
  whole file, and the tool then quietly starts asking for an administrator password. That is why
  scripts/sec-gate.sh validates the generated file against the DTD and then asks polkitd what it
  actually parsed.

  There is deliberately no org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.allow_gui annotation. It exists to let a
  privileged program open an X connection, and a root process on the user's display is precisely
  what this design removed.
-->
<policyconfig>

  <vendor>CPU-Power</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/rations/cpu-power</vendor_url>

  <action id="@CPUPOWER_POLKIT_ACTION_ID@">
    <description>Adjust CPU frequency scaling and power settings</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to adjust CPU frequency scaling and power settings</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>yes</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">@CPUPOWER_HELPER_PATH@</annotate>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.argv1">@CPUPOWER_PKEXEC_ARGV1@</annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
